WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.090
Everyone knows the lengths to which Marvel
Studios and Avengers: Endgame directors Joe

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:09.560
and Anthony Russo have gone to prevent any
spoilerific leaks from flooding the internet.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.459
There's the now-standard sneaky editing of
trailers to include and exclude certain footage,

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:19.610
and reports of the Brothers Russo crafting
purposefully confusing days on set — but

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.940
now we're learning another technique the Endgame
team implemented to protect the film and keep

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.280
everyone in the dark until the movie's premiere.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.140
Bruce Banner actor Mark Ruffalo recently revealed
that he shot five different endings for Avengers:

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.740
Endgame — and for a pretty hilarious reason.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.820
Chatting with E! News alongside his co-stars
Chris Evans and Karen Gillan Ruffalo admitted

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.780
that he has absolutely no idea how Endgame
truly ends, or what really happens in the

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.230
movie… because he never got a complete script.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.200
"I don't know what's gonna happen.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.640
I shot, like, five different endings to this
movie.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.550
I didn't even get a whole script to this movie.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.340
And I don't know why."

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.440
He added that the version of the Endgame script
he did receive, quote, "had dummy scenes in

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.440
it," one of which included Evans' Captain
America tying the knot.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:07.560
Mark's a real liability.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.160
"He gets married in this!"

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.020
They have to throw Mark off the scent."

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.520
Flabbergasted, Ruffalo asked Evans why Marvel's
braintrust would knowingly steer him in the

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.520
wrong direction.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.700
"You have earned no trust in the Marvel universe.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.180
You, like, leak like a bodily function."

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.180
"Like I need a diaper?"

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.620
It turns out that the majority of the Endgame
cast was given the same treatment as Ruffalo:

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.280
they shot the film with incomplete scripts.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.060
Another member of the Endgame crew that wasn't
entrusted with the full story?

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:44.539
The notorious spoiler-spiller Tom Holland,
who knows even less about what goes down in

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:45.900
Endgame than Ruffalo.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.299
The Hulk star at least had mock-up scenes
to read and fake endings to film, but Holland

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.020
wasn't awarded such a luxury.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.740
Director Joe Russo previously explained that
the Spider-Man actor only had access to his

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:02.200
lines and nothing else — for the very same
reason Ruffalo was kept uninformed and made

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.140
to shoot a bunch of different conclusions.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.480
He said at a fan event for the film in India,

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.800
"Tom Holland does not get the script.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.840
Tom Holland gets his lines and that's it.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.599
He doesn't even know who he's acting opposite
of … we use, like, very vague terms to describe

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:19.420
to him what is happening in the scene, because
he has a very difficult time keeping his mouth

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:19.920
shut."

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.400
In defense of Marvel and the Russo brothers'
tactics, it's true that Ruffalo hasn't exactly

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:28.620
been the best gatekeeper of the studio's secrets,
having accidentally let slip information that

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.600
was meant to be guarded as if his life depended
on it.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:37.400
In the past, Ruffalo unintentionally live-streamed
to his Instagram followers the first 20 minutes

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.660
of audio from Thor: Ragnarok during the film's
Los Angeles world premiere back in 2017.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.160
And who can forget the time Ruffalo completely
spoiled the ending of Avengers: Infinity War

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.580
during an interview with ABC's Good Morning
America?

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.640
“Wait til you see this next one.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:52.720
Everybody dies."

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:53.220
"Dude!

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:53.720
Dude…

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:55.160
“Not everybody"

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.280
Make no mistake about it: Marvel is serious
about shielding secrets — now more so than

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:05.780
ever.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.680
As the film approaches its April 26 launch
date, the studio is going to crack down on

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.060
potential spoiler-sharers.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.180
So remember, kids, keep those lips buttoned
until everyone has a chance to see Endgame...or

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.260
else face the wrath of Thanos.

